# Mini-cloud manifest reference

## Application layout

Create one flat child directory:

```text
{{.AppsDir}}/my-app/
├── mini-cloud.json
├── executable-or-script
├── .env                 # optional
└── data/                # optional; normally exclude mutable data from watching
```

The directory name is the application name and, by default, becomes `<name>.{{.BaseDomain}}`.

## Runtime selection

Select exactly one runtime:

- `process`: Start an HTTP server on its first request and stop it after its idle timeout. Listen on `127.0.0.1:$PORT`. Mini-cloud provides `PORT` and `LISTEN_ADDRESS`; `${PORT}` and other environment values may be interpolated in command arguments.
- `static`: Serve files directly from a configured subdirectory.
- `cgi`: Start one RFC-style CGI command per request.

### Process example

```json
{
  "access": "authenticated",
  "process": {
    "command": ["./server", "--port", "${PORT}"],
    "working_directory": ".",
    "environment_files": [".env"],
    "readiness": { "type": "http", "path": "/health" }
  },
  "idle": "5m",
  "watch": { "ignore": ["data", "tmp"] }
}
```

### Static example

```json
{
  "access": "public",
  "static": {
    "root": "public",
    "spa_fallback": "index.html",
    "browse": false
  }
}
```

### CGI example

```json
{
  "access": "authenticated",
  "cgi": {
    "command": ["./handler"],
    "working_directory": ".",
    "timeout": "30s",
    "maximum_concurrency": 4
  }
}
```

## Common top-level fields

- `host`: Optional hostname override.
- `display_name`: Human-friendly label for the app directory at `{{.IndexHost}}`.
- `description`: Short text shown on the app tile.
- `icon`: App-relative image path used by the tile. Mini-cloud reads it directly without waking a process app; keep the path inside the app directory.
- `access`: `public` or `authenticated`; defaults to authenticated.
- `idle`: Process idle timeout; `"0s"` disables idle shutdown.
- `watch.ignore`: Relative file or directory patterns that should not trigger restarts. Exclude databases, caches, generated files, and other mutable state.
- `cron`: Scheduled commands using five-field cron expressions. Each entry has `name`, `schedule`, `command`, optional `timeout`, `overlap` (`forbid`, `allow`, or `replace`), and `missed` (`skip`). Cron commands do not keep the HTTP process awake.

## Tile metadata

```json
{
  "display_name": "Kitchen display",
  "description": "Status and controls for the kitchen.",
  "icon": "assets/favicon.svg"
}
```

SVG, PNG, ICO, and other browser-supported image formats work. Prefer a square image. The icon is served at `/icons/<app-name>` on the app directory host; do not construct that URL manually or point `icon` at an external URL.

## Commands, environment, and diagnostics

Environment files use simple `KEY=VALUE` lines and are not shell scripts. Commands are argument arrays and do not implicitly run through a shell. Use an explicit `[/bin/sh, -c, ...]` command only when shell behavior is required.

Application stdout and stderr go to the mini-cloud systemd journal. Runtime state, manifest errors, assigned ports, restart counts, and cron results are visible at <https://{{.AdminHost}}>.
